skill: "turn-ideas-into-reality"
sections[9]:
  - "\n"
  - "# Turn Ideas into Reality (TTM Framework)\n\nThis skill codifies the Time to Materialize (TTM) framework for rapidly transitioning concepts from ideation into validated real-world execution.\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## What is TTM?\n\nTTM stands for **Time to Materialize**—the duration between conceiving an idea and putting a tangible version into the real world.\n\n- **Validation Over Perfection**: The initial objective of any idea is not perfection; it is empirical validation.\n- **Execution Generates Clarity**: Elaborate planning without market contact creates paralysis. Real-world feedback produces the data required to decide what to build next.\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## The 3-Step TTM Framework\n\n"
  - "### Step 1: Reverse Engineer the Outcome\n\nStart with the end result, not the implementation process or toolchain.\n\n1. **Identify the Smallest Proof of Potential**:\n   - Ask: *What is the single smallest result that proves this idea has real potential?*\n   - Examples:\n     - **Software & SaaS**: Securing the first paying customer or signed letter of intent.\n     - **Content Creation**: Publishing the first post that secures audience engagement or a brand inquiry.\n     - **Consulting & Services**: Booking the first paid discovery session.\n     - **Digital Products**: Processing the first live transaction.\n2. **Eliminate Premature Systems**:\n   - Do not build support infrastructure, automation pipelines, or administrative tooling until the single proof metric is demonstrated.\n\n"
  - "### Step 2: Make It Small & Execute (The 24-Hour Constraint)\n\nApply the 24-hour constraint to force radical scope reduction:\n\n1. **The Forcing Question**:\n   - *If this had to happen in the next 24 hours, what would I do?*\n2. **Build the Smallest Possible Version**:\n   - Instead of building a complex multi-page website -> Deploy a single landing page with a direct call to action.\n   - Instead of building an entire multi-week curriculum -> Host and sell a single live workshop.\n   - Instead of planning a 100-video calendar -> Record, edit, and publish video number one.\n   - Instead of incorporating an agency -> Pitch one target client directly.\n3. **Ship and Measure**:\n   - Deploy the minimal asset into public view immediately.\n\n"
  - "### Step 3: Compress Your Feedback Loop\n\nGrowth velocity is bounded by how quickly real-world learning occurs.\n\n1. **The Iteration Cycle**:\n   ```\n   Execute → Gather Feedback → Change One Variable → Test Again\n   ```\n2. **Single-Variable Testing**:\n   - When an experiment underperforms, isolate and modify exactly one variable (for example, the opening hook, the pricing threshold, or the headline) before deciding whether to keep or discard the core premise.\n3. **Minimize Cycle Duration**:\n   - Shorten cycle times from weeks to hours to run more experiments within the same timeframe.\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## The 2-Hour Attention System (Content Application)\n\nWhen applying TTM to content, distribution, and media growth, use the structured attention pipeline:\n\n1. **Validated Topic Ideation**: Identify topics that address real audience friction or curiosity rather than speculative brainstorming.\n2. **Scroll-Stopping Hooks**: Craft the opening 3 seconds to define the core tension and reward for the viewer.\n3. **Direct Script Architecture**: Transition immediately from hook into tension, deliver the core insight, and conclude with an actionable takeaway without narrative drift.\n4. **Lean Capture and Publishing**: Record and edit with minimal tool overhead to maintain publishing cadence.\n5. **Metric-Driven Refinement**: Inspect drop-off points in retention curves, change one element in the next iteration, and test again.\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## Operational Checklist\n\nBefore writing code, designing interfaces, or producing content under this framework:\n\n- [ ] Has the target outcome been reverse-engineered to its smallest proof metric?\n- [ ] Has the implementation been scoped down to what can run or ship within 24 hours?\n- [ ] Have all unnecessary infrastructure layers and premature abstractions been removed?\n- [ ] Is there an active feedback mechanism to measure real-world response?\n- [ ] When iterating, is only a single variable being modified per cycle?\n"
